    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$3.44 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a skillet dinner with fruit, vegetables and protein already in it. There is nothing else to cook and nothing else to buy.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy apples loose, not in the bag.\u003c/strong\u003e Bagged apples are usually smaller and priced by convenience. Buying loose lets you pick firm ones that hold up to heat, and it is often cheaper per apple.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eA firm tart apple is the right apple.\u003c/strong\u003e Softer sweet varieties turn to sauce in the pan. You want something that keeps its shape when it cooks.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCabbage is doing most of the volume work.\u003c/strong\u003e A couple of heads fill the pan for very little. That is why this dish feeds fourteen the way it does.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eApple juice and dijon cost almost nothing per batch.\u003c/strong\u003e A bottle of juice and a jar of mustard both last for months, and together they are what makes this taste finished rather than plain."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Slice the smoked sausage into coins, slice the onion, core and shred the cabbage, and core and slice the apples thick.",
                    "Heat the olive oil in your largest skillet and brown the sausage coins hard on both sides, then set them aside.",
                    "Cook the sliced onion in the sausage fat until it softens and picks up the color from the pan.",
                    "Add the cabbage in batches, letting each handful wilt before the next goes in, and cook until it turns tender and sweet.",
                    "Add the apple slices and cook just a few minutes. You want them softened but still holding their shape, not collapsed.",
                    "Whisk the chicken broth, apple juice, dijon mustard, apple cider vinegar and dried thyme together, pour it into the skillet, and simmer until it reduces and glazes everything.",
                    "Return the sausage, season with salt and black pepper, and cook the rice separately to serve underneath."
                ],
